evidence.statement:
# 4. Layer 1 — Event Detection

BMC's automation portfolio includes event-driven automation.

BMC AMI Ops Automation documents rules that can automatically respond to system events, including:

- suppressing messages;
- generating automatic replies;
- generating alerts;
- sending events into other BMC event-management components.

Source:

https://docs.bmc.com/xwiki/bin/view/Mainframe/Ops/BMC-AMI-Ops-Automation/bao8301/Getting-started-with-BMC-AMI-Ops-Automation/Product-features-and-key-concepts-of-BMC-AMI-Ops-Automation/Basic-automation/

# 7. Layer 4 — Recommendation / Next Action

BMC's AI assistant documentation states that after identifying a root cause, the assistant can suggest:

- remediation steps;
- configuration changes;
- automation opportunities.

Recommendations can be tailored to:

- historical data;
- system/event logs;
- best practices;
- organizational policies;
- workflows.

Source:

https://docs.bmc.com/xwiki/bin/view/Mainframe/Common/BMC-AMI-Platform/amip23/FAQs-and-additional-resources/

# 8. Layer 5 — Tool Invocation

BMC's current MCP gateway allows administrators to register MCP servers and expose their tools.

BMC documents:

- MCP server registration;
- tool discovery;
- tool assignment to namespaces;
- prompts;
- connection testing;
- OAuth authentication;
- role-based namespace access.

The documentation states that in Assist mode, BMC AMI Assistant can dynamically invoke tools exposed by registered MCP servers.

Source:

https://docs.bmc.com/xwiki/bin/view/Mainframe/Common/BMC-AMI-Platform/amip23/Using/Using-Platform-Manager/MCP-gateway/

# 11. Layer 8 — Action Execution

BMC AMI Ops Automation explicitly documents automatic corrective actions.

It can:

- collect information about problems as they happen;
- take corrective actions automatically;
- coordinate management of system objects;
- start and stop procedures;
- maintain requested object states;
- distribute workloads to meet performance or disaster-handling goals.

Source:

https://docs.bmc.com/xwiki/bin/view/Mainframe/Ops/BMC-AMI-Ops-Infrastructure/baoi71/Getting-started/Key-concepts/Automation-and-object-management/

# 12. Layer 9 — Rule-Based Automation

BMC AMI Ops Automation provides a Rule Processor for event automation.

Rules can:

- suppress messages;
- provide automatic replies;
- generate alerts;
- send events to BMC Impact Manager components.

Source:

https://docs.bmc.com/xwiki/bin/view/Mainframe/Ops/BMC-AMI-Ops-Automation/bao8301/Getting-started-with-BMC-AMI-Ops-Automation/Product-features-and-key-concepts-of-BMC-AMI-Ops-Automation/Basic-automation/

# 13. Layer 10 — Policy-Driven Automation

BMC's monitoring policy architecture allows conditions and actions to be combined into automated policies.

Policies can include:

- agent policies;
- object policies;
- expressions;
- policy actions.

Source:

https://docs.bmc.com/xwiki/bin/view/Mainframe/Ops/MainView-Middleware-Monitor/MVMM92/Getting-started/User-goals-and-features/

# 14. Layer 11 — State-Oriented Automation

BMC documents automated IPL templates where a system progresses through defined phases/states.

Each state can contain:

- commands;
- rules;
- timeout values;
- error actions.

The automation starts based on an event, operator action or scheduled task and selects the appropriate initial state based on system status.

Source:

https://docs.bmc.com/xwiki/bin/view/Mainframe/Ops/BMC-AMI-Ops-Console-Management-BMC-AMI-Ops-Console-Automation-and-BMC-AMI-Ops-SecureHMC/AMICMCASH41/Using/Using-the-Configurations/Console-Automation/IPL-templates/

# 17. Native Baseline Is Stronger Than Initially Assumed

OPP-01-01 initially treated the gap as potentially existing between:

```text
AI RECOMMENDATION
        ↓
EXECUTION
```

The current research shows that this gap is **not automatically present**.

BMC already has:

- tool invocation;
- MCP;
- OpenAPI tool conversion;
- rule-based automation;
- corrective actions;
- policy-driven automation;
- human validation;
- state-based execution;
- event-driven automation.

# 22. II-009 — Tool Registry as Execution Infrastructure

BMC's MCP gateway centralizes:

- server registration;
- tools;
- prompts;
- namespaces;
- authentication;
- authorization;
- connection testing.

Source:

https://docs.bmc.com/xwiki/bin/view/Mainframe/Common/BMC-AMI-Platform/amip23/Using/Using-Platform-Manager/MCP-gateway/

# 8. BMC Has Workload Trade-Off Controls

BMC Control-M Workload Policies provide another relevant example.

Workload Policies can:

- limit concurrent jobs;
- limit resources;
- route jobs between hosts;
- apply rules to groups of jobs.

Source:

https://docs.bmc.com/xwiki/bin/view/Control-M-Orchestration/Control-M/workloadautomation/Control-M-Automation-API/ctmapi9211/Services/Run-service/Workload-Policies-reference/

# 14. BMC Can Dynamically Reallocate Capacity

BMC AMI Ops Automation for Capping dynamically reallocates CPU capacity based on optimization policies.

BMC states that iCap can:

- manage and prioritize workloads;
- selectively distribute MSUs;
- increase capacity for high-priority workloads;
- enforce capacity limits.

Source:

https://docs.bmc.com/xwiki/bin/view/Mainframe/Ops/BMC-AMI-Ops-Automation-for-Capping/opsacap31/Getting-started/

